[38;5;231m⏺[39m Stopping before the edit — this one has a consequence beyond the two lines.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is the shared client, and [38;5;153mBASE_URL[39m is just an env var. Both scripts import it:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py:5[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py:5[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m (nightly finance export)

  So [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m turns off TLS certificate validation for the [1mproduction[0m run as well,
  not just staging. That makes the nightly export of finance data vulnerable to an active man-in-the-middle: anything
  that can intercept the connection can present any certificate and both read and alter the report data. Python would
  also emit [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m on every call.

  The self-signed staging cert is a real problem, but this fix doesn't stay inside staging.

  Two ways to keep it scoped, if you want either:

  1. [1mEnv-driven verify[0m — [38;5;153mVERIFY = os.environ.get("REPORTS_CA_BUNDLE") or os.environ.get("REPORTS_VERIFY", "1") != "0"[39m,
     passed as [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY[39m. Production keeps verification on by default; [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m sets the opt-out alongside
     the base URL it already sets.
  2. [1mPin the staging CA[0m — point [38;5;153mverify[39m at the staging cert's PEM file. Verification stays on everywhere; staging just
     trusts its own cert.

  Tell me which you want (or confirm you really do want it off for production too) and I'll make the change.

